---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Driftstack"
  description="Pixel-identical iPhone Safari sessions in the cloud. Bit-identical canvas, WebGL, audio, and user-agent — no runtime patching, no stealth plugins, nothing for detection to find. API, SDK, or GUI. Try it for $2.99."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 grid-bg opacity-40" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-28 pt-24 md:pt-32">
      <div class="grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center">
        <div class="animate-fade-up">
          <p class="section-label">iPhone Safari · on demand</p>
          <h1
            class="mt-5 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            Pixel-identical iPhone Safari. Cloud-hosted. API, SDK, or GUI.
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Spin up a session in seconds. Drive it from TypeScript, Python,
            Go, or any HTTP client — or skip the code entirely and use the
            GUI. Every signal — user-agent, canvas, WebGL, audio
            fingerprint — is bit-identical to a physical iPhone running
            the same iOS Safari build.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Most stealth tools rewrite JavaScript at runtime and leak the
            underlying engine in the next hash. Driftstack builds from
            Apple's WebKit source directly. Nothing is rewritten, so there's
            nothing for detection systems to spot.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
            <a href="/pricing#trial-pack" class="btn-primary">Start for $2.99</a>
            <a href="/comparison" class="btn-secondary">Why not Browserless?</a>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-ink-muted">
            16 hours of session time · 14-day window · one trial per account.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="code-preview">
          <div class="code-window-chrome">
            <span class="pip"></span>
            <span class="pip"></span>
            <span class="pip"></span>
            <span class="ml-3 text-xs text-ink-muted">driftstack-typescript-sdk</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-5 text-ink-primary"><code>{`import { Driftstack } from '@driftstack/sdk';

const ds = new Driftstack({ apiKey: process.env.DRIFTSTACK_API_KEY });

const session = await ds.sessions.create({
  archetype: 'iphone16pro_ios18_7_safari26_4',
  proxy: { type: 'wireguard', config: '...' },
});

await session.navigate({ url: 'https://target.example' });
const challenge = await session.waitForChallenge();
await session.interact({ tap: '#submit' });

const state = await session.getState();
await session.destroy();`}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- F-4 (Issue 2) — "two ways to drive it" section. The hero
       above shows the imperative SDK; this section pairs it with
       the AI-agent shape per file 06 agent-layer spec. Stacked
       vertically (no JS-driven tab switcher) so the page stays
       static-prerender clean and Cloudflare Pages-cacheable. The
       AI-agent code uses createWithAgent + an NL task so the
       customer's mental shift from imperative → declarative is
       obvious at a glance. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <p class="section-label">Two ways to drive it</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
      >
        Imperative SDK above. Sentence-driven agent below.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        Need pixel-precise control? Drive intents directly (the hero
        example). Need fast iteration on a new flow? Describe the
        task in English; the agent layer decomposes it into the same
        intents under the hood. Same session API, same archetype,
        same fingerprint — just two ways in.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 code-preview">
        <div class="code-window-chrome">
          <span class="pip"></span>
          <span class="pip"></span>
          <span class="pip"></span>
          <span class="ml-3 text-xs text-ink-muted">driftstack-typescript-sdk · agent layer</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-5 text-ink-primary"><code>{`import { Driftstack } from '@driftstack/sdk';

const ds = new Driftstack({ apiKey: process.env.DRIFTSTACK_API_KEY });

// Describe the task in English. The agent layer decomposes it into
// navigate / fillForm / click / screenshot intents and walks the
// session through them — with a live preview you can watch.
const session = await ds.sessions.createWithAgent({
  archetype: 'iphone16pro_ios18_7_safari26_4',
  proxy: { type: 'wireguard', config: '...' },
  task: 'Log in with the supplied credentials, navigate to the analytics dashboard, and screenshot the revenue widget.',
  credentials: { username: process.env.TARGET_USER, password: process.env.TARGET_PASS },
});

await session.run();              // agent decomposes + executes
const result = await session.result();
//   → { screenshots: [...], transcript: [...], intents: [...] }`}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-ink-muted">
        Click <strong>Get code</strong> in the agent UI to export the
        decomposed intent log as a Direct API script — the agent
        becomes a one-shot scaffold; recurring runs skip the agent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- F-2 / F-5 (Issue 4 + 5 + 6 + 7) — replaces the prior R7
       compliance-badges 4-up strip (EU-resident / Stripe-billed /
       GDPR DPA / Source escrow) with product-differentiator signals.
       Compliance copy lives on /trust + /legal pages and on the
       footer meta-link row; the homepage's splash slot below the
       hero is for differentiators a competitor can't claim. The
       prior aspirational-launch eyebrow was stripped per Issue 5
       (launch-only framing — no "Pre-launch" labelling). -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-10">
      <p class="text-center font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-muted">
        What sets us apart
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">Bit-identical</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">Canvas · WebGL · audio · UA</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">Three proxies</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">SOCKS5 · WireGuard · OpenVPN</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">Three access paths</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">API · SDK · GUI</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">EU-hosted</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">Single-region data residency</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Use cases (new — production polish: social proof + concrete uses) -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <p class="section-label">Use cases</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
      >
        Anywhere mobile Safari fidelity decides the outcome.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">QA + testing</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-ink-primary">Mobile-Safari coverage</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Run end-to-end tests against the exact engine your iOS users
            run. Same rendering, same JavaScript timing, same quirks. No
            "works on my Mac, fails on iPhone" surprises in production.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">Data collection</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-ink-primary">Mobile-first scraping</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Many sites serve different content to mobile Safari than to
            desktop Chrome. With Driftstack, your scraper sees what an
            iPhone visitor would see — no behind-the-scenes redirects to
            "please use our app" pages.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">Account workflows</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-ink-primary">Multi-account operations</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Keep separate identities on services that lock down by device
            fingerprint. Persistent profiles let each account look like
            its own physical phone, every session, every time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- R8 — Integrations strip. Outseta-style "works with your stack"
       row. Three SDK languages + four orchestration tools that
       customers commonly slot Driftstack into. Honest framing: only
       the 3 SDKs are first-party; the orchestration tools (Playwright,
       n8n, Make, Zapier) work because we expose a plain HTTPS API,
       not because we ship integration plugins. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">Works with your stack</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            Three SDKs. One HTTPS API. Slots into anything.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            First-party SDKs in TypeScript, Python, and Go. Beyond that, any
            HTTP-aware tool can drive Driftstack — Playwright tests, n8n
            workflows, Make.com scenarios, Zapier triggers, plain
            <code class="font-mono text-glow-red">curl</code> from a cron job.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-center md:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">SDK</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">TypeScript</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">@driftstack/sdk</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">SDK</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">Python</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">driftstack-sdk</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">SDK</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">Go</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">driftstack-go</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">via API</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">Playwright</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">drives sessions</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">via API</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">n8n · Make</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">workflow nodes</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">via API</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">curl · cron</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">plain HTTPS</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The detection problem (renamed from "Cumulative rig" — too jargony) -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="section-label">Why Driftstack works</p>
      <p
        class="mt-6 text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.04em;"
      >
        <span class="bg-gradient-to-br from-ink-primary via-ink-primary to-glow-red bg-clip-text text-transparent">
          Indistinguishable from a real iPhone.
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        Detection systems read hundreds of small signals — screen size,
        font rendering, GPU details, JavaScript timing — to decide what
        kind of device is visiting. Driftstack matches every signal
        against a reference iPhone, exactly. Not approximate, not "within
        tolerance".
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        Reference device: iPhone 16 Pro, iOS 18.7, Safari 26.4. If any
        measurable signal differs from what that phone sends, we treat
        it as a launch-blocking bug and fix the engine — not the JavaScript
        wrapper around it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Full signal-by-signal methodology lands on
        <code class="font-mono text-glow-red">/trust/cumulative-rig</code> shortly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- R8 — Fingerprint check visual. Replaces the previous text-only
       "we match every signal" claim with a side-by-side stub showing
       what a real iPhone vs. a typical stealth browser returns. The
       6-row table is a stand-in for the full /trust/cumulative-rig
       signal table; concrete numbers make the claim visually tangible
       instead of asking the prospect to trust prose. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-start">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">What detection systems see</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            Same signals as a physical iPhone. Not "close enough".
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Detection systems read hundreds of small signals. A typical
            stealth browser fakes the user-agent string but leaks the
            underlying engine in the canvas, WebGL, or audio fingerprint.
            Driftstack runs Apple's WebKit, so every signal returns the
            exact iPhone value — not approximate.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-muted">
            A 6-signal preview. Full signal-by-signal table lands on
            <code class="font-mono text-glow-red">/trust/cumulative-rig</code>
            shortly — see
            <a
              href="/trust/security-overview"
              class="text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-glow-red-soft"
              >/trust/security-overview</a
            >
            for what's live today.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card overflow-hidden">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-[1fr_auto_auto] items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3 px-6 py-5 text-sm">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">
              Signal
            </span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">
              Stealth Chromium
            </span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">
              Driftstack
            </span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">User-agent</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">spoofed</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">real</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">Canvas hash</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks Chromium</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">iPhone</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">WebGL renderer</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks Chromium</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">Apple GPU</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">AudioContext</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks Chromium</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">iOS</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">Core Text metrics</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks system</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">iOS</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">JS engine timing</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">V8 fingerprint</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">JSCore</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Stack — full-width statement -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="section-label">How it's built</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-5xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
      >
        Apple's engine. Not a Chromium copy.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        Every stealth tool you've used before is a Chromium fork with a
        fake user-agent, or Playwright with a patch plugin layered on
        top. Driftstack runs Apple's WebKit source code itself — the
        same engine that ships on every iPhone. WebKit, Core Text, and
        the iOS rendering pipeline produce your fingerprint the way
        Apple wrote them, in the order Apple intended.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Metering — single dense paragraph + data point -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <div class="grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] md:items-end">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">Metering</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            One metric. Concurrent sessions. That's it.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink-primary">Concurrent</strong> means
            sessions running at the same time — think browser tabs. Pick a
            plan, get a concurrent cap, run anything inside it. No
            per-call markup. No per-element fees. No hourly metering that
            turns idle sessions into surprise overage charges. Visit
            200 pages on one session for the cost of visiting one.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="accent-rule flex flex-col gap-1">
          <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">
            Concurrent cap
          </span>
          <span
            class="font-mono text-5xl font-semibold text-ink-primary"
            style="letter-spacing: 0.02em;"
          >
            =
          </span>
          <span class="text-sm text-ink-secondary">the only thing you pay for</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Compliance — 60/40 split with code-style egress block -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <div class="grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-5 md:items-start">
        <div class="md:col-span-3">
          <p class="section-label">Privacy + compliance</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            Customer data stays in the EU.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Database, object storage, and compute all run in the EU,
            single-region. We log session metadata only — when a
            session ran, how long, which device archetype. The pages
            your session visits are never stored or read by the
            control plane. Outbound traffic can route through proxy
            regions you choose; per-vendor sub-processor breakdown
            at
            <a href="/trust/sub-processors" class="text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-glow-red-soft"
              >/trust/sub-processors</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Customer-configurable egress (SOCKS5 / WireGuard / OpenVPN)
            — see
            <a href="/trust/security-overview" class="text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-glow-red-soft"
              >/trust/security-overview</a
            >
            for the current implementation state and the security
            posture across each proxy type.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md:col-span-2">
          <div class="code-preview p-5">
            <span class="text-ink-muted"># Driftstack control plane</span><br />
            <span class="arrow-bullet">→</span> stores session metadata only<br />
            <span class="arrow-bullet">→</span> never stores response bodies<br />
            <span class="arrow-bullet">→</span> EU-jurisdiction object storage<br />
            <br />
            <span class="text-ink-muted"># Roadmap — customer-configurable egress</span><br />
            <span class="text-ink-muted">→ SOCKS5 / WireGuard / OpenVPN (not shipped)</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Built for two audiences -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <div class="mb-10">
        <p class="section-label">Two ways to use it</p>
        <h2 class="mt-6 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary md:text-4xl" style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
          Drive it by hand, or drive it from code.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-8">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">Manual</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink-primary">Desktop app</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            A native desktop client that lets you keep 10 to 200 logged-in
            profiles open at once and switch between them like browser
            tabs. Same WebKit, same fingerprint, no code required. Built
            for account teams and operators who keep multiple sign-ins
            alive across the workday.
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-5 space-y-2.5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="arrow-bullet">→</span>
              <span>Solo Manual $79/mo · Team $249/mo · Agency $699/mo</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="arrow-bullet">→</span>
              <span>1 / 3 / 8 concurrent sessions per tier</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="arrow-bullet">→</span>
              <span>Unlimited hours within your concurrent cap</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <a href="/pricing#manual" class="btn-secondary mt-7 inline-flex">See Manual pricing →</a>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-8">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">API</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-ink-primary">SDK + HTTPS</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            First-party SDKs in TypeScript, Python, and Go — plus a
            plain HTTPS API any HTTP client can drive. Built for QA
